  DEFENDING ARMY

  The attackers had the 340th Warg Riders and the 301st Eagles in reserve.  
Negumba led his armies courageously into the battle.  Jolin marched with his 
loyal troops, jaws set in grim resolution.
  Negumba's men, ill-supplied in the desert campaign, suffered from thirst.  
Jolin's men, ill-supplied for the tortuous desert, suffered from thirst.  
Negumba's luck didn't hold up this time.  His superstitious men became 
unsettled when his priests were unable to properly sacrifice a bull to the 
Great Father because they had all been stolen by gryphons.  On the other 
hand in this perilous slugging match, Negumba seemed to have the tactical 
edge.
  Negumba was frustrated by the Eagles, who virtually ignored his military 
strategy.
  Across the entire front, the 125th Phalanx faced the 204th Axemen.  The 
134th Warg Riders and the 136th Warg Riders flanked the enemy and attacked 
with full strength.  The 301st Eagles soared over the 204th Axemen and 
attacked them.  In this area, the 125th Phalanx were being steadily driven 
back.  But then the 125th Phalanx's commander wielded its pike and 
personally held off all assailants, and the 125th Phalanx rallied admirably. 
The 125th Phalanx battled with the 204th Axemen and stout-heartedly advanced 
against them.
  The battle ended in victory for the soldiers of Negumba as they broke 
through the lines at a critical moment.  The 134th Warg Riders gave chase 
and slew the 204th Axemen almost to a man.
  Negumba's campaign accomplished the conquest of Apurimac.  The battle 
affected the quality of our forces:  we could not have been better satisfied 
with the fighting spirit of the 125th Phalanx, and we have elevated its 
ranking to Veteran.  The 134th Warg Riders fought determinedly during the 
battle and came through with a good effort.  We can now rank it a Veteran 
unit.

  Thy humble servant,   Montgomery, Squire to Negumba


